Done. Here's what I did:

1. **Added two new filter regexes** next to ERROR_LINE_RE:
   • PASSING_TEST_RE — matches `test some::path ... ok` lines (passing Rust tests whose names contain error-like words)
   • TEST_RESULT_OK_RE — matches `test result: ok.` summary lines (which mention "0 failed" but aren't errors)

2. Applied the filters in collect_failed_ci_fingerprints() — after a line matches `ERROR_LINE_RE`, it's checked against both filters. ANSI codes are stripped before the check so colored CI output is handled correctly. Lines matching either filter are skipped.

3. Added 6 self-tests covering:
   • Passing test with "error" in name → filtered ✓
   • FAILED test with "error" in name → kept ✓
   • Real compiler error → kept ✓
   • test result: ok. summary → filtered ✓
   • test result: FAILED. summary → kept ✓
   • Passing test with ANSI color codes → filtered ✓

This eliminates the `[4×] test watch::tests::test_watch_result_failed_with_error ... ok` false positive while preserving detection of actual CI errors.
